# Numeric literals must be read as a whole.
#
# REGRESSION: the parser used to rebuild a literal out of the pieces the grammar
# splits off — `(integer as i32 as f64 + ".frac".parse()) * 10^exponent`. Every
# line below was wrong because of it.

# The integer part went through i32, so anything past 2^31 fell off entirely.
INPUT A1 =3000000000
CHECKNUM A1 3000000000

# ...and here only the fraction survived: this used to be 0.5.
INPUT A2 =12345678901.5
CHECKNUM A2 12345678901.5

INPUT A3 =123456789012345678/1000000000
CHECKNUM A3 123456789.012

# The exponent was applied as a multiply, so a literal near the top of the range
# overflowed to infinity — and inf/inf is not 1.
INPUT A4 =1E308/1E308
CHECKNUM A4 1

# ...and one near the bottom flushed to zero, making this #DIV/0!.
INPUT A5 =1E-320/1E-320
CHECKNUM A5 1

# Adding the two halves rounds twice, which put 4.778 one ulp above the
# correctly rounded double. One ulp in an exponent is not small: this MOD over
# a large power came out 0.263671875.
INPUT A6 =MOD(-(566.5^4.778),1)
CHECKNUM A6 0.34375
